    "complementary": {
      "label": "Complementary",
      "readings": [
        {
          "role": "first",
          "ref": "Deuteronomy 6:1-9",
          "refKey": "Deut.6.1-Deut.6.9",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "covenant",
              "law-and-commandment",
              "love-of-neighbor"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "exhortation"
            ],
            "function": [
              "creed-affirmation"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 6,
              "v_start": 5,
              "v_end": 5,
              "text": "And thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thine heart — And is this only an external commandment? Can any then say, that the Sinai - covenant was merely external? With all thy heart - With an entire love. He is One; therefore our hearts must be united in his love. And the whole stream of our affections must run toward Him. O that this love of God may be shed abroad in our hearts."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 6,
              "v_start": 7,
              "v_end": 7,
              "text": "Teach them diligently — Hebrew. whet, or sharpen them, so as they may pierce deep into their hearts. This metaphor signifies the manner of instructing them, that it is to be done diligently, earnestly, frequently, discreetly."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 6,
              "v_start": 8,
              "v_end": 8,
              "text": "Thou shalt bind them — Thou shalt give all diligence, and use all means to keep them in thy remembrance, as men often bind something upon their hands, or put it before their eyes to prevent forgetfulness of a thing which they much desire to remember."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "psalm",
          "ref": "Psalm 119:1-8",
          "refKey": "Ps.119.1-Ps.119.8",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "faithfulness",
              "law-and-commandment",
              "word-of-god"
            ],
            "image": [
              "road-and-journey"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "trust"
            ],
            "function": [
              "illumination-word"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 119,
              "v_start": 6,
              "v_end": 6,
              "text": "Respect — A due respect, which implies hearty affection, diligent study, and constant practice. To all - So as not to allow myself in any known sin, or in the neglect of any known duty."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 119,
              "v_start": 7,
              "v_end": 7,
              "text": "When — When by thy good spirit I shall he more fitly instructed in the meaning of thy word."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 119,
              "v_start": 8,
              "v_end": 8,
              "text": "Forsake me not — For then I shall fall into the foulest sins."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "second",
          "ref": "Hebrews 9:11-14",
          "refKey": "Heb.9.11-Heb.9.14",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "atonement",
              "mercy-and-forgiveness",
              "passion-and-cross"
            ],
            "image": [
              "blood",
              "lamb"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "assurance",
              "wonder-and-awe"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 9,
              "v_start": 11,
              "v_end": 11,
              "text": "An high priest of good things to come — Described, ver. 15. Entered through a greater, that is, a more noble, and perfect tabernacle - Namely, his own body. Not of this creation - Not framed by man, as that tabernacle was."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 9,
              "v_start": 12,
              "v_end": 12,
              "text": "The holy place — Heaven. For us - All that believe."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 9,
              "v_start": 13,
              "v_end": 13,
              "text": "If the ashes of an heifer — Consumed by fire as a sin-offering, being sprinkled on them who were legally unclean. Purified the flesh - Removed that legal uncleanness, and re-admitted them to the temple and the congregation. Num. xix, 17, 18, 19."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 9,
              "v_start": 14,
              "v_end": 14,
              "text": "How much more shall the blood of Christ. — The merit of all his sufferings. Who through the eternal Spirit - The work of redemption being the work of the whole Trinity. Neither is the Second Person alone concerned even in the amazing condescension that was needful to complete it. The Father delivers up the kingdom to the Son; and the Holy Ghost becomes the gift of the Messiah, being, as it were, sent according to his good pleasure. Offered himself - Infinitely more precious than any created victim, and that without spot to God. Purge our conscience - Our inmost soul. From dead works - From all the inward and outward works of the devil, which spring from spiritual death in the soul, and lead to death everlasting. To serve the living God - In the life of faith, in perfect love and spotless holiness."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "gospel",
          "ref": "Mark 12:28-34",
          "refKey": "Mark.12.28-Mark.12.34",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "kingdom-of-god",
              "law-and-commandment",
              "love-of-neighbor"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "exhortation"
            ],
            "function": [
              "creed-affirmation"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 12,
              "v_start": 28,
              "v_end": 28,
              "text": "Which is the first commandment? — The principal, and most necessary to be observed. Matt. xxii, 34; Luke x, 25."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 12,
              "v_start": 29,
              "v_end": 29,
              "text": "The Lord our God is one Lord — This is the foundation of the first commandment, yea, of all the commandments. The Lord our God, the Lord, the God of all men, is one God, essentially, though three persons. From this unity of God it follows, that we owe all our love to him alone. Deut. vi, 4."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 12,
              "v_start": 30,
              "v_end": 30,
              "text": "With all thy strength — That is, the whole strength and capacity of thy understanding, will, and affections."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 12,
              "v_start": 31,
              "v_end": 31,
              "text": "The second is like unto it — Of a like comprehensive nature: comprising our whole duty to man. There is no other moral, much less ceremonial commandment, greater than these. Lev. xix, 18."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 12,
              "v_start": 33,
              "v_end": 33,
              "text": "To love him with all the heart — To love and serve him, with all the united powers of the soul in their utmost vigour; and to love his neighbour as himself - To maintain the same equitable and charitable temper and behaviour toward all men, as we, in like circumstances, would wish for from them toward ourselves, is a more necessary and important duty, than the offering the most noble and costly sacrifices."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 12,
              "v_start": 34,
              "v_end": 34,
              "text": "Jesus said to him, Thou art not far from the kingdom of God — Reader, art not thou? then go on: be a real Christian: else it had been better for thee to have been afar off."
            }
          ]
        }
